"""A public overview that accurately separates new measurements and source audit."""
import html,json,pathlib
ROOT=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2];D=ROOT/'docs';DATA=D/'evaluation'
E=lambda x:html.escape(str(x))
s=json.loads((DATA/'summary.json').read_text())
complete=all(x['status']=='complete' for x in s.values())
cards=''
for m,name in [('luna','GPT-6-Luna'),('sol','GPT-6.1-Sol')]:
 r=s[m]; rate=f'{r["primary_annotation_matches"]}/{r["primary_annotation_denominator"]}' if r['primary_annotation_denominator'] else 'Pending'
 cards+=f'<section class="card"><span class="tag">New reviewer evaluation · Extra High</span><h3>{name}</h3><p><strong>{r["papers_reviewed"]}/27 papers reviewed</strong><br><strong>{rate}</strong> specific annotations matched<br>{r["total_flags"]} reviewer flags reported</p><p>One fresh review per manuscript. Raw flags, reference matching and run provenance are available for every case.</p><a class="button" href="{m}/matrix.html">Explore {name.split("-")[-1]} results</a><div class="smalllinks"><a href="{m}/matrix.advanced.html">Advanced</a> · <a href="evaluation/{m}/results.json">Results JSON</a></div></section>'
paired='<p><a href="evaluation/comparison.html">Compare the same papers side by side →</a> · <a href="evaluation/paired-results.csv">Download paired CSV</a></p>' if complete else ''
interpretation=''
if complete:
 l=s['luna'];o=s['sol'];lm=l['primary_annotation_matches'];sm=o['primary_annotation_matches']
 finding=f'Luna matched {lm} of 12 specific reference annotations; Sol matched {sm} of 12.'
 if lm<12 and sm<12: finding+=' Both left some specific reference annotations unmatched under this text-only setup.'
 elif lm<12 or sm<12: finding+=' One reviewer left some specific reference annotations unmatched under this text-only setup.'
 else: finding+=' Both matched every reference in this small primary subset.'
 interpretation='<p><strong>'+finding+'</strong> These automated coverage results address one part of the original question: detection of scientific errors. An older-model control was not run on these same inputs, so this pilot does not establish improvement over GPT-4 or reproduce the older studies’ other findings.</p>'
status='Both model runs and automated matching are complete.' if complete else 'Evaluation is in progress. Counts below represent completed work, not final results.'
header='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="author" content="Richardson Dackam"><meta name="description" content="A new text-only pilot evaluating GPT-6-Luna and GPT-6.1-Sol as scientific manuscript reviewers, alongside an archived audit of 27 studies."><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"><title>Can newer models detect scientific errors? | Nshipyard Research</title></head><body><header><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a class="brand" href="index.html">Nshipyard / Research</a><div class="navlinks"><a href="#results">New results</a><a href="evaluation/methods.html">Methods</a><a href="literature.html">Literature audit</a><a href="https://github.com/richardsondx/llm-peer-review-evidence">GitHub ↗</a></div></nav></div></header><main class="wrap">'''
body=f'''<section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Scientific peer review · Exploratory evaluation</div><h1>Can newer models detect scientific errors?</h1><p class="lead">GPT-6-Luna and GPT-6.1-Sol review 27 manuscripts directly, using identical text-only inputs. Their newly generated critiques are compared with published SPOT error annotations.</p><p class="byline">By <a href="https://richackam.com">Richardson Dackam</a> · Independent researcher, <a href="https://nshipyard.com">Nshipyard</a><br>1 October 2026 · Version 0.3.0 · Exploratory research · Not peer reviewed</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="#results">Explore the new results</a><a class="button secondary" href="evaluation/methods.html">Read the evaluation protocol</a>{('<a class="button secondary" href="evaluation/report.pdf">Read the research report (PDF)</a>' if complete else '')}</div><div class="status"><strong>{status}</strong><p>These are new reviews by Luna and Sol. The earlier evidence extraction remains available as a separate <a href="literature.html">literature audit</a>.</p></div></section>
<section class="section prose"><h2>The question behind this project</h2><p>I wanted to test whether concerns raised in an earlier review of AI peer review—especially missed scientific errors—still apply to newer models. Re-reading old papers cannot answer that performance question. This evaluation asks the newer models to act as reviewers themselves.</p><p>The original review and its newer-model source audits cover 27 published studies. The new experiment uses a <strong>different set of 27 manuscripts from SPOT</strong>. These are two separate research components, not the same 27 experiments rerun.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="results"><h2>New reviewer results</h2><p>Both models use Extra High reasoning. Luna runs first, then Sol. Each manuscript receives one independent review per model, without supplied error annotations or browsing.</p><div class="grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr))">{cards}</div>{paired}<p>“Matched” means an automated judge found the same specific error mechanism at a compatible location. It does not establish that the reference annotation or reviewer claim is scientifically correct.</p></section>
<section class="section prose"><h2>What the pilot can tell us</h2>{interpretation}<p>The primary measure is coverage of <strong>12 sufficiently specific reference annotations</strong>. All 27 papers and 29 selected-category annotations remain visible; vague references and a spelling-only issue are excluded from the primary denominator. The full screening record is available.</p><p>The six matrix columns show reference detection, primary coverage, reviewer flags, self-reported confidence, reference quality, and input limitations. They describe this evaluation rather than borrow measurements from earlier studies.</p><p>Unmatched flags may identify valid additional issues. The pilot has no clean-paper controls, human reviewer comparison or independent expert adjudication. It therefore cannot estimate hallucination rate, score bias, confidence calibration, or whether either model is ready for autonomous peer review.</p></section>
<section class="section prose"><h2>Read the evidence, not just the score</h2><p>Each cell opens the original annotations, the complete model critique, the matching rationale and the recorded run. Public benchmark exposure, text-only figure omission and single-run variability limit interpretation. A shared automated judge also creates a potential source of scoring bias. These coverage counts cannot be compared directly with the original SPOT headline rates: the corpus, text-only inputs, reference screen, prompt and scoring procedure differ.</p><a href="evaluation/methods.html">Methods and limitations →</a> · <a href="evaluation/protocol-amendment.json">Primary execution protocol</a> · <a href="evaluation/annotation-audit.json">Annotation screening</a></section>
<section class="section prose"><h2>The literature baseline</h2><p>The original Keenable review is preserved unchanged, alongside Luna and Sol’s audits of its source claims. It provides historical context, not a controlled GPT-4 reviewer run on this pilot. Its GPT-4 generator attribution remains unconfirmed.</p><a href="baseline/matrix.html">Original evidence matrix</a> · <a href="literature.html">27-study literature audit</a> · <a href="comparison.html">Compare extraction versions</a> · <a href="report.html">Literature report</a></section>
<section class="section prose" id="cite"><h2>Citation and reuse</h2><p>Cite a specific repository version and state that these results are exploratory and AI-adjudicated. The archived literature report remains separately citable as version 0.2.0. No DOI, journal acceptance or Google Scholar indexing is claimed.</p><div class="citation">Dackam, R. (2026). <em>Can newer models detect scientific errors? A text-only reviewer pilot with GPT-6-Luna and GPT-6.1-Sol.</em> Nshipyard technical report NS-LLMPR-2026-01. <a href="https://github.com/richardsondx/llm-peer-review-evidence/releases/tag/v0.3.0">Version 0.3.0 and reproducible records</a>.</div><div class="smalllinks"><a href="CITATION.cff">Citation metadata</a> · <a href="citation.bib">BibTeX</a> · <a href="https://github.com/richardsondx/llm-peer-review-evidence/issues">Suggest a correction</a></div></section></main><footer><div class="wrap">© 2026 Richardson Dackam · <a href="https://nshipyard.com">Nshipyard</a> · <a href="https://x.com/richardsondx">X @richardsondx</a><br>AI-assisted exploratory research. Original studies and benchmark annotations retain their own authorship and rights.</div></footer></body></html>'''
(D/'index.html').write_text(header+body)
protocol=json.loads((DATA/'protocol.json').read_text())
methods='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css"><title>Reviewer pilot — Methods and limitations</title></head><body><main class="wrap"><article><a href="../index.html">Research overview</a><h1>Reviewer pilot: methods and limitations</h1><p>Author: Richardson Dackam · Nshipyard · 1 October 2026. This is an exploratory text-only adaptation of <a href="https://llms-in-science.github.io/spot/">SPOT</a>, not an official SPOT replication.</p><h2>Design and sample</h2><p>27 unique manuscripts were selected by round-robin over five non-image annotation categories, in alphabetical title order within each category: equation/proof, statistical reporting, reagent identity, experiment setup and text–text inconsistencies. The sample contains 16 equation/proof papers, four statistical-reporting papers, three reagent-identity papers, two experiment-setup papers and two text–text inconsistency papers. This enriches it for equation/proof problems. The input is the repository’s normalized text, with each image replaced by an explicit omission marker. All selected-category annotations for each sampled paper are retained (29 total); other-category annotations are outside scope. There are 223 image omissions across the inputs.</p><p>These 27 manuscripts are distinct from the 27 studies in the literature audit. We do not claim to reproduce the experimental designs of those studies.</p><h2>Execution amendment and supplementary runs</h2><p>An initial CLI route completed 27 Luna reviews, but the Sol CLI requests were rejected before inference. The primary comparison was therefore moved to a consistent app sub-agent interface for both models, using fresh reviews. The earlier Luna CLI outputs and rejected Sol requests remain <a href="supplementary/luna-cli/results.json">supplementary records</a> and do not enter the paired primary scores. The change was recorded before any primary app reviews existed in the <a href="protocol-amendment.json">execution amendment</a>.</p><h2>Model execution</h2><p>Requested runtime identifiers: <code>gpt-6-luna</code> and <code>gpt-6.1-sol</code>, both <code>xhigh</code>. Both primary runs use fresh app sub-agents with no inherited conversation per manuscript. The paper and schema can be read, and the output can be saved; browsing, annotations and other reviews are prohibited. Compliance is self-attested, not independently event-audited. Two manuscript reviewers can run concurrently within each model; Luna’s entire primary run precedes Sol’s. All receive the same fixed manuscript text, embedded reviewer prompt and JSON schema. Per-worker orchestration instructions specify paths and tool restrictions; public task templates document these instructions. Full app request envelopes are not independently audited. No annotations, peer outputs or source-audit results are supplied. These are app sub-agent measurements; runtime identifiers are selected in the spawn request, not independently attested API snapshot IDs. Tool restrictions and full-input reading are recorded as worker attestations.</p><p>Each manuscript receives one review per model. No regeneration is selected for higher scores. Failures and runs using prohibited tools are invalid, must remain documented, and cannot count as successful reviews.</p><h2>Reference screening and scoring</h2><p>The full annotation set remains visible. The primary denominator contains 12 references that describe a sufficiently specific error mechanism. Generic statements that a named theorem, table or experiment is flawed are excluded, as is the spelling-only reference outside the prompt’s substantive-error scope. Screening was saved after execution began, before eligible-case outputs were inspected; the P03 output had already been viewed to verify runtime behavior. This is not an external preregistration.</p><p>Each paper’s two outputs are assigned A/B labels with order determined by a hash of its case ID. A fresh GPT-6.1-Sol Extra High app sub-agent compares both to the references without reviewer model names. A match requires the same specific faulty mechanism and a compatible location. Generic criticism or shared topic is insufficient. The judge produces a short rationale and prediction indexes for each match. Scoring is automated and needs independent human validation; a Sol-family judge may introduce bias despite withheld model identities.</p><p>The headline reports matched specific annotations divided by the 12 eligible annotations. Broad reference matching is descriptive only. Neither reference matching nor a reviewer’s “established error” label verifies scientific truth. The annotations themselves may be incomplete or incorrect.</p><h2>Measures intentionally unavailable</h2><p>Without clean-paper controls and independent verification of every flag, we cannot estimate true precision, false-positive or hallucination rates. Without human reviewers or scoring tasks, we cannot measure human agreement, human-level performance or score bias. Self-reported confidence is displayed but is not confidence calibration. A failure to match a reference does not prove that the model found no valid scientific issue.</p><h2>Reproducibility and rights</h2><p>The manifest records paper identifiers, input SHA-256 hashes, source revision, text lengths and omitted-image counts. Raw reviewer JSON, run configuration, worker attestations, automated matching and screening rationales are downloadable. Full third-party manuscript text and private local runtime logs are excluded from publication; scripts rebuild inputs from the public SPOT repository. The annotation dataset is attributed to SPOT’s authors and <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/amphora/SPOT-MetaData">SPOT-MetaData</a>. Third-party material retains its own license.</p><p>The initial CLI execution protocol was saved before those reviewer outputs. The app execution amendment was saved before any primary app review outputs. The selection and scoring decisions are versioned in the repository; they are not a registered clinical or systematic-review protocol.</p><h2>Benchmark citation</h2><p>Son, G. et al. (2025). <em>When AI Co-Scientists Fail: SPOT-a Benchmark for Automated Verification of Scientific Research.</em> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11855">arXiv:2505.11855</a>. <a href="benchmark.bib">BibTeX</a>.</p><h2>Machine-readable records</h2><p><a href="README.md">Data dictionary</a> · <a href="app-reviewer-task-template.txt">Reviewer task template</a> · <a href="app-judge-task-template.txt">Matching task template</a> · <a href="matching-execution-amendment.json">Matching execution amendment</a></p><ul><li><a href="report.html">Research report (HTML)</a> · <a href="report.pdf">PDF</a></li><li><a href="protocol.json">Execution protocol</a></li><li><a href="cases.json">Case manifest and annotations</a></li><li><a href="annotation-audit.json">Reference screening and timing</a></li><li><a href="summary.json">Current aggregate counts</a></li><li><a href="luna/results.json">Luna reviews and matches</a></li><li><a href="sol/results.json">Sol reviews and matches</a></li></ul><h2>Initial CLI protocol, amended for the primary app runs</h2><pre>'''+E(json.dumps(protocol,indent=2))+'''</pre></article></main></body></html>'''
(DATA/'methods.html').write_text(methods)
print('New evaluation overview and methods rendered')
